00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:55.280
So, if you're new to the table, this looks a little different in here.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:57.280
It's because we're in the month of July.

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:02.280
And in the month of July, we give our staff the practice of a salad,

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:05.280
where they get to take a rest and prepare for the next year.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:14.280
And so, the joy of that for us is we get to have people who don't normally get to lead,

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.280
whether it be through music or through preaching.

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:20.280
So, thank you again to Gary and Noel.

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:29.280
So, is anybody here a fan of basketball, reading, or coffee?

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:33.280
Oh, great! You have something in common with our guest speaker tonight.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:34.280
Yay!

00:23:34.280 --> 00:23:36.280
So, we're going to be hearing from Greg Steele.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:39.280
He and his wife, Tiffany, have been married for 16 years.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:44.280
They have two cute kiddos, and they have called the table home for about a year and a half.

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:47.280
So, they're thrilled to introduce you to Greg.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:52.280
Applause

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:54.280
Hey, thanks for being here, everybody.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:24:00.280
Especially want to welcome those who haven't been here before or who are watching online.

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:04.280
Because of what was just said, let you know that I'm not the minister.

00:24:04.280 --> 00:24:06.280
I'm just a member here.

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:10.280
So, if you are someone who watches online frequently,

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:16.280
you've no doubt noticed the kids who are loud and wild every time we dismiss for kids' worship.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:18.280
And you've probably wondered, like, whose kids are those?

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:20.280
Where are those kids' parents?

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.280
It's me.

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:24.280
I'm those kids' dad.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.280
But my name's Greg. My pronouns are he, him.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:30.280
And I'm really glad you all chose to be here today.

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:36.280
In 1903, an excerpt from a poem by Emma Lazarus, titled The New Colossus,

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:40.280
was engraved on the pedestal of the Statue of Liberty.

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:42.280
In full, the poem reads,

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:45.280
Not like the brazen giant of Greek fame,

00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:49.280
With conquering limbs astride from land to land,

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:52.280
Here at our sea-washed sunset gate shall stand

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.280
A mighty woman with a torch whose flame

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:58.280
Is the imprisoned lightning in her name.

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:01.280
Mother of exiles, from her beacon hand

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:05.280
Glows worldwide welcome her mild eyes command

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:09.280
That air-bridged harbor the twin cities frame.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:14.280
Keep, ancient lands, your storied pomp, cries she with silent lips.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:18.280
Give me your poor, your huddled masses yearning to breathe free,

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:21.280
The wretched refuse of your teeming shore.

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:24.280
Send these, the homeless, tempest-tossed to me.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:27.280
I lift my lamp beside the golden door.

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:33.280
The Colossus of Rhodes, the title refers to,

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:36.280
is one of the so-called seven wonders of the ancient world.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:41.280
It was a 100-foot tall bronze statue of the Greek sun god Helios

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:45.280
that stood on the shore of a port city named Rhodes.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:48.280
And there was an inlet coming in from the sea

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:50.280
that went between the statue's legs.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:54.280
So a very imposing presence, right?

00:25:55.280 --> 00:25:59.280
It was a symbol of imperial might, of the power,

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:02.280
of one of the strongest nations in the world at the time.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:07.280
But to the poet, it is not the same kind of sculpture

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:10.280
as the Statue of Liberty.

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:13.280
To the poet, the Statue of Liberty or Lady Liberty

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:16.280
is conceived of as the mother of exiles,

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:20.280
the one who will welcome the poor, the destitute,

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:22.280
the homeless into the U.S.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:24.280
And you can see this even in the language she uses

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:28.280
to describe holding a lantern by the golden door.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:31.280
It's almost reminiscent of like an innkeeper

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:34.280
welcoming tired travelers in the middle of the night

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:38.280
or a lighthouse casting a flickering beam across choppy waves

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:41.280
to help the ships get into shore.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:46.280
So this poet conceives of the Statue of Liberty

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:49.280
and by extension the United States in some ways

00:26:49.280 --> 00:26:53.280
as a welcome, a mother of exiles.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:27:01.280
And that has maybe never been less true than it is now.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:05.280
In the United States of today,

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:08.280
everybody who could be described as among

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.280
you're tired, you're poor, the homeless,

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:13.280
tempest-tossed is in very grave danger.

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:18.280
Migrants and immigrants who the poem was referring to

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:22.280
most directly originally are in particular danger

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:26.280
of seizure and bodily harm, including deportation,

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:28.280
unlawful detention, and worse.

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:32.280
Everywhere around us there are poor families

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:37.280
losing access to health care or SNAP benefits or both.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:40.280
There are members of the LGBTQIA community

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:43.280
facing daily harassment

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:47.280
and powerful people working to prepare concentration camps.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:50.280
On a personal level, my family is really worried

00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:54.280
and I would say fearful about the intentions

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:56.280
of the Department of Health and Human Services

00:27:56.280 --> 00:28:00.280
collecting information on people with autism.

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:04.280
So suffice it to say there's a lot of people hurting,

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:07.280
deeply hurting, and many of us in this community

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:11.280
have been personally victimized by these injustices.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:14.280
And look, can we be honest?

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:16.280
Let's name this.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:20.280
I do not have the experience to talk to this room

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.280
about oppression.

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:27.280
I am a cisgender, heterosexual, white man in the United States,

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:30.280
which means I was born as part of the people group

00:28:30.280 --> 00:28:34.280
who hasn't been under the boot of oppression

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:38.280
and has instead enjoyed or experienced privilege

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:41.280
in many ways and many times.

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.280
Lots of people in our community have experienced

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:46.280
and are experiencing oppression

00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:52.280
that I couldn't really fully comprehend.

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:55.280
What I do know is that it hurts

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:58.280
and that we are all hoping for a lamp beside the golden door

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:01.280
or even just a light at the end of the tunnel,

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:03.280
some sign that things will be better,

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:06.280
things could be better in the future than they are now,

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:11.280
that it isn't a permanent darkness.

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:15.280
And that sense of kind of being in a permanent darkness,

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:18.280
I find grows in recent years.

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:21.280
I remember at the beginning of the pandemic,

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:23.280
people started saying these are unprecedented times.

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:26.280
But then we all kept saying it, right?

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:28.280
Like it's always unprecedented.

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:31.280
And for some people, it's become kind of a joke, right?

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:35.280
Like how many historical events am I expected to live through

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:39.280
per year, you know, before something lets up?

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:41.280
And I want to name that,

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:45.280
but even though these are unprecedented times,

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:48.280
in times that are like this,

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:52.280
I find myself reaching out for Jesus.

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:55.280
Jesus was part of the people group

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:58.280
that lived under the shadow of an empire.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:01.280
It was the empire which conquered the people

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:05.280
who built that 100-foot tall colossus statue.

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:08.280
Those of you who grew up in church

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:11.280
or grew up hearing Bible stories may already know this,

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:15.280
but it bears repeating that Jesus grew up

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:19.280
and lived in a world where Roman soldiers

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:21.280
could force people to carry their baggage,

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:27.280
where the tax collection service could bleed people dry

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:32.280
and funnel all the revenue back to fill the pockets of the empire,

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:37.280
where people could be executed as political dissidents.

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:41.280
Jesus himself was executed as a political dissident.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:43.280
Now, you're going to stop me and say,

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.280
that's all there is to it.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:49.280
Jesus' death had religious significance.

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:51.280
It was a sacrifice.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:53.280
It meant more than that,

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:56.280
and I'm not trying to dispute any of that.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:58.280
All I want to focus our attention on

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.280
is that the charge he was tried under

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:02.280
was being a political dissident,

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.280
and the posted notice at Jesus' execution

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.280
said he was a political dissident.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:10.280
It said, Jesus of Nazareth, King of the Jews.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:14.280
So, of course, his death meant much, much more than that.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:16.280
But one of the things it meant

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:21.280
was that his world was a world where dissidents could be executed.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:25.280
So in times like our times,

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:27.280
in these unprecedented times,

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:32.280
it's maybe more timely to hear the words and deeds of Jesus

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:34.280
than at any other time.

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:39.280
I think maybe Jesus' message is for unprecedented times,

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:41.280
as we call it.

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:45.280
His people, the people that Jesus was born into,

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:48.280
were not only facing oppression by the empire,

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:50.280
the Roman Empire,

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:54.280
but they were also in the midst of a spiritual crisis of sorts.

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:57.280
There were a lot of different ways,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:01.280
different ideas about what it meant to be a faithful Jew,

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:03.280
a true Jew.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:07.280
And some of those ways were really difficult or even impossible

00:32:07.280 --> 00:32:10.280
given their setting in the world.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.280
Their society, Jewish society,

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:16.280
was a tiny corner of that huge empire.

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:19.280
And it was part of a cosmopolitan society,

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.280
one of the first melting pots,

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:24.280
if you like that phrase or are familiar with that.

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:28.280
And so some ideas of being a good servant of God,

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:29.280
being a good Jew,

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:33.280
included isolationism from non-Jews

00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:36.280
that most people just couldn't do, right?

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:39.280
You can't afford to be picky about where your food comes from

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:44.280
if you don't have a lot of money to buy Jewish food.

00:32:44.280 --> 00:32:50.280
You can't afford to be particular about working or not working on some days

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:54.280
if you've got to get along, got to get by to survive.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:32:59.280
And within the many levels of power and privilege,

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:02.280
Jesus identified that the majority of Jews,

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.280
the majority of people that he lived around and knew,

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:08.280
were having a burden imposed on them,

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:11.280
not only by the empire,

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:15.280
but also by the elite of their own society,

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:18.280
the religious establishment such as it was.

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:23.280
That rings true for many of us, I think.

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:29.280
I think many of the members of this community have joined it

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:33.280
because we've seen that Christians and Christianity

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:37.280
can be the force that's oppressing people

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:41.280
who are at the bottom of the social hierarchy in our world.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:44.280
Like, it's partly because of Christians,

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.280
or mostly because of Christians,

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:48.280
that women can't access abortions.

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:51.280
It's partly because of Christians

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:53.280
that there are so many attempts to write bathroom laws

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:56.280
that are intended to hurt transgender people.

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:59.280
Those are examples that come to mind for me.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:03.280
There may be other examples that resonate more with you,

00:34:03.280 --> 00:34:06.280
but I think you get the idea, right?

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:10.280
And, of course, one of the reasons we're here

00:34:10.280 --> 00:34:13.280
is because we believe that it's not all Christians.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:18.280
And we believe that it's not real, true Christianity that does those things.

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:23.280
But that distinction helps to clarify

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:28.280
why our unprecedented times link up with Jesus' time so much.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:32.280
Because, for us, it's true.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:33.280
It's not all Christians.

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:36.280
It's not real Christianity that's doing those things.

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:40.280
But it's the Christians that have power, right?

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:43.280
That have political and social clout,

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:46.280
who are part of the lawmaking process,

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:48.280
who are causing those things.

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.280
And that was true for Jesus' people, too.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.280
It wasn't all Jews who believed those things

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:59.280
or who were causing the people on the bottom to suffer so much.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:01.280
But it was the Jews who had power,

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:05.280
the ones who held positions in the temple, the religious center,

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:09.280
who had political and social clout.

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.280
His unprecedented times were a lot like ours, actually.

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:17.280
So part of the meaning of what Jesus did and taught

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:22.280
was that it happened in the context of oppression and exploitation.

00:35:22.280 --> 00:35:26.280
He and his friends, his family, his people

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:30.280
lived their lives in the face of overwhelming pressure

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:37.280
from the empire and from within, from the temple elite.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:41.280
In fact, one time Jesus was visiting his hometown

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:44.280
and he was asked to do the scripture reading

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:46.280
during worship service.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:49.280
And he actually chose a scripture

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:52.280
that was all about proclaiming good news to the poor,

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:54.280
liberation for captives, freedom for the oppressed,

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:56.280
healing for the blind.

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:59.280
And it's sort of informative for what he thought was important

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:01.280
about what he would do,

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:05.280
because he began his remarks about that passage by saying,

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:09.280
today these words are fulfilled in your hearing.

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:14.280
So against that backdrop, in that setting,

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:17.280
Jesus said some words that are recorded for us

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:20.280
in Matthew chapter 11, verses 28 through 30.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:23.280
He said, come to me, all you who are weary

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:26.280
and are carrying heavy burdens, and I will give you rest.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:29.280
Take my yoke upon you and learn from me,

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:31.280
for I am gentle and humble in heart,

00:36:31.280 --> 00:36:33.280
and you will find rest for your souls.

00:36:33.280 --> 00:36:36.280
For my yoke is easy and my burden is light.

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:41.280
There was an idiom in Jewish culture at the time

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:48.280
that taking someone's yoke upon you meant learning from them

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:50.280
or following in their way of life,

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:54.280
a little bit similar to how we might say a mentor

00:36:54.280 --> 00:36:56.280
or somebody is going to take you under their wing

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.280
or show you the ropes.

00:36:58.280 --> 00:37:01.280
And this idiom was most commonly used, right,

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:03.280
in reference to God's law,

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:05.280
that God's law is like a yoke

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:08.280
that's going to teach us how to live.

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:15.280
But in Jesus' mind, it was kind of a perfect metaphor

00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:17.280
for himself and his people.

00:37:17.280 --> 00:37:21.280
There was a heavy burden, like draft animals plowing a field,

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:25.280
row after row, year after year, they plowed, they plowed,

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:29.280
with that yoke on their backs to guide them endlessly,

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:32.280
but it was tough to bear.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:39.280
And so Jesus' offer to those people was rest for their soul.

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:46.280
That rest meant welcoming people

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:52.280
who were on the outside looking in of the religious center.

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:56.280
Jesus' offer for them was to belong

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:58.280
as part of the people of God,

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:00.280
to come to Jesus' own table,

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:02.280
to welcome in the people who'd been left out

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:05.280
or kicked out or screened out or forced out.

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:10.280
The burden that weighed them down involved

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:13.280
or included a very specific idea

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:15.280
of what it meant to be one of the people of God.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:17.280
And the reason that was a burden

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:21.280
is because if you can't fit that very specific boundary,

00:38:21.280 --> 00:38:26.280
then you're on the outs in your own society,

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:28.280
an outcast.

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:33.280
And so one of the biggest ways Jesus lifted that burden

00:38:33.280 --> 00:38:35.280
was by opening his arms.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:37.280
He offered rest to everyone,

00:38:37.280 --> 00:38:40.280
not just the people with a qualifying burden,

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:43.280
but anybody with a burden.

00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:47.280
Indeed, Jesus' welcome was a type of yoke

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:50.280
or a way of taking people under his wing,

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:52.280
as we might say.

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:54.280
He was replacing the heavy yoke

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:57.280
with a different type of guidance.

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:59.280
He welcomes all his listeners

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:02.280
into a way of being guided through the field

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:05.280
that is not based on having

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:10.280
a heavy two-by-four across our back.

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:15.280
It's a light tugging and a gentle burden to bear,

00:39:15.280 --> 00:39:17.280
and it's an invitation to find rest.

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:22.280
But what is that rest for us?

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:30.280
Jesus invites everyone to find rest in him

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:35.280
because he is gentle and humble in heart.

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:38.280
And that fills me with some hope,

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:40.280
a restful sense.

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:43.280
Because it means that in the face

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:46.280
of unimaginable oppression,

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:53.280
there is a response that doesn't need anger as fuel.

00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:58.280
Even in the face of unimaginable oppression,

00:39:58.280 --> 00:40:00.280
there could be a response

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:04.280
that doesn't need anger as fuel.

00:40:04.280 --> 00:40:06.280
There's a resistance to injustice

00:40:06.280 --> 00:40:08.280
that is gentle and humble in heart.

00:40:08.280 --> 00:40:12.280
I find that most days, nowadays,

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:14.280
I wake up in the morning

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:16.280
wishing that there were some way

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:19.280
to make the world right.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:22.280
Wishing that governments would be forced to do justice,

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:26.280
that people would be made to condemn bigotry,

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.280
and so on.

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:30.280
In my heart of hearts,

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:32.280
I respond to the violence

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:35.280
that's used to carry out this oppression

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:39.280
with a kind of violence of my own.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:41.280
Meeting force with force.

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:45.280
And there is a need to be forceful

00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:48.280
when working for justice.

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:49.280
For my part though,

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:54.280
I find that it is a restless way of living

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:57.280
to rely only on that force.

00:40:57.280 --> 00:40:59.280
To have that be the only fuel.

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:02.280
It becomes a heavy burden

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:04.280
to always have to have that anger

00:41:04.280 --> 00:41:07.280
as the only thing fueling me.

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:08.280
An additional burden,

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:11.280
even adding on to how terrible things are.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:18.280
Have any of you felt that burden?

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:22.280
But Jesus offers rest for our souls

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:24.280
through learning from Him.

00:41:24.280 --> 00:41:26.280
And we're free to learn from Him

00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:30.280
because He is gentle and humble in heart.

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:34.280
It might mean that the real rest

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:37.280
is recognizing that compassion

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:40.280
is what can overcome hatred.

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:44.280
That returning hatred with my own hatred

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:46.280
can never undo hatred.

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:54.280
It can only increase that burden on our backs.

00:41:54.280 --> 00:41:56.280
In the rest Jesus offers us,

00:41:56.280 --> 00:42:00.280
we face the ugliness of our moment in history

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:03.280
with overflowing compassion.

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:05.280
When our souls are at rest,

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:07.280
when we've received that rest,

00:42:07.280 --> 00:42:09.280
we're able to remember that courage

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:12.280
shows itself in unyielding love

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:15.280
just as much as in unyielding force.

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:19.280
And I don't know if there are any history nerds out there.

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:21.280
And I'm definitely no expert.

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:24.280
But one thing we've seen a lot of

00:42:24.280 --> 00:42:26.280
in the history of the United States

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:30.280
has been the power of unyielding love

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:32.280
more than the power of unyielding force.

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:34.280
In the American Civil Rights Movement,

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:38.280
some of the major moves forward

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.280
were made by Ruby Bridges, a schoolgirl

00:42:40.280 --> 00:42:46.280
who endured incredible insults and physical violence

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:48.280
to integrate a school.

00:42:48.280 --> 00:42:50.280
And Rosa Parks, an older woman

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:52.280
who went through a lot

00:42:52.280 --> 00:42:55.280
for refusing to get out of a seat on the bus

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:58.280
after a long shift.

00:42:58.280 --> 00:43:01.280
And even the efforts of the suffragettes

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:04.280
to gain women voting rights

00:43:04.280 --> 00:43:06.280
happened not because the suffragettes

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:11.280
had weapons to force the polls to allow them to vote,

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:13.280
but because they had a courage

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:18.280
and an unyielding devotion to justice

00:43:18.280 --> 00:43:20.280
that brought them through

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:23.280
and that caused the world that we're in now.

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:28.280
Recently, a Superman movie came out

00:43:28.280 --> 00:43:30.280
in the last couple of weeks.

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:31.280
I don't know if you've seen it,

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:35.280
but if you do, I want you to remember this

00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:36.280
when you hear one line.

00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:41.280
During the movie, Superman is talking with Lois Lane,

00:43:41.280 --> 00:43:44.280
who is a brilliant reporter

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:46.280
and the two of them have been dating

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:48.280
for like a short while at this time.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:52.280
And she says, I'm punk rock

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:56.280
and you're, you know, a hokey small town guy.

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:58.280
And then goes on to say,

00:43:58.280 --> 00:44:00.280
like he sees the best in people

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:03.280
even though they don't have much of a best.

00:44:03.280 --> 00:44:07.280
And Superman says, maybe kindness is the real punk rock.

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:10.280
And that kind of occurred to me

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:13.280
in thinking about this, right?

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:16.280
That the real strength of like,

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:18.280
even a superhero like Superman

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:21.280
isn't that he can just punch people harder.

00:44:21.280 --> 00:44:24.280
It's that he can keep on loving,

00:44:24.280 --> 00:44:29.280
can keep on being devoted to justice,

00:44:30.280 --> 00:44:35.280
can maintain that love, that compassion.

00:44:35.280 --> 00:44:38.280
In some ways, I even feel like

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.280
that learning from Jesus,

00:44:40.280 --> 00:44:42.280
being taken under Jesus' wing,

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:45.280
that is the rest.

00:44:45.280 --> 00:44:47.280
As in, the rest for our souls

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:49.280
is what happens in the process

00:44:49.280 --> 00:44:52.280
of trying to learn from Jesus.

00:44:52.280 --> 00:44:56.280
I haven't found that rest, I have to tell you.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:59.280
I seem to let being at rest depend too much

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:01.280
on what's going on around me.

00:45:01.280 --> 00:45:04.280
And with the way things are right now,

00:45:04.280 --> 00:45:06.280
it's hard for me to feel much rest at all

00:45:06.280 --> 00:45:08.280
in that way.

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:10.280
But Jesus was able to offer an invitation

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:12.280
to come and rest.

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:15.280
Even as part of an oppressed people group.

00:45:15.280 --> 00:45:18.280
He offers the invitation to learn

00:45:18.280 --> 00:45:20.280
how to be gentle and humble in heart

00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:25.280
even under the most desperate circumstances.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:30.280
And to find rest in doing so.

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:33.280
I wonder also if part of the rest

00:45:33.280 --> 00:45:35.280
Jesus welcomes us to

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:38.280
is also a grace that sets us free

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.280
from feeling like we need

00:45:41.280 --> 00:45:45.280
to meet violence with violence.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:48.280
Because as I reflect

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:51.280
on the promise of rest for our soul,

00:45:51.280 --> 00:45:53.280
there is a persistent feeling that

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.280
the point of that rest,

00:45:55.280 --> 00:45:57.280
the purpose of that rest

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:59.280
is not for me to get energy

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:01.280
to do more violence.

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:03.280
The purpose of the rest

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:07.280
is to give me, give us

00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:09.280
the energy to fight and resist

00:46:09.280 --> 00:46:10.280
in the right ways.

00:46:10.280 --> 00:46:13.280
To teach us how to resist.

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:15.280
My daughter is five,

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:19.280
so we recently have to stop calling it nap time

00:46:19.280 --> 00:46:21.280
and start calling it rest time.

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:24.280
So like being at rest in our house

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:27.280
is when Hannah is having her rest time.

00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:30.280
And when she talks about this,

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:31.280
she often says,

00:46:31.280 --> 00:46:33.280
I'm going to have rest time

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:36.280
so I can be full of injury later.

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:41.280
And that means energy.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:46.280
But that's what's got me thinking

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:48.280
that this rest for our souls

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:49.280
that Jesus is talking about

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:53.280
is not for the purpose of having injury inside us

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:56.280
and spreading that injury after our rest.

00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:59.280
It's for filling us with energy

00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:07.280
and spreading that energy outside of us.

00:47:07.280 --> 00:47:10.280
Jesus stands beside the golden door

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:12.280
with a lamp raised in His hand.

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:16.280
He invites us all

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:18.280
and bids us welcome.

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:20.280
He welcomes all those

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:22.280
who are beaten down and burdened

00:47:22.280 --> 00:47:24.280
to come in and rest,

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:27.280
no matter what the burden is.

00:47:27.280 --> 00:47:30.280
He seeks out the huddled masses

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:32.280
on teeming shores

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:35.280
that are written about on the Statue of Liberty

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:37.280
and brings them in.

00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:40.280
He welcomes everyone who is poor, homeless,

00:47:40.280 --> 00:47:42.280
and tempest-tossed.

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:44.280
He stretches out His hands

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:48.280
the people suffering oppression in our world

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:50.280
and to those who have been harmed

00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:53.280
by religious figures and institutions

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.280
everywhere all over.

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:59.280
He welcomes us in as a host and a guide.

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:01.280
He offers to show us how to live,

00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:03.280
to take us under His wing

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:06.280
and guide us in our path.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:08.280
And we can accept that.

00:48:08.280 --> 00:48:10.280
We can trust Him

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:13.280
because we know that He is gentle and humble in heart.

00:48:13.280 --> 00:48:15.280
And when we do,

00:48:15.280 --> 00:48:18.280
we get welcomed into rest.

00:48:18.280 --> 00:48:21.280
Rest for our souls.

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:24.280
Rest that faces even the greatest darkness

00:48:24.280 --> 00:48:29.280
with a power, an inextinguishable power.

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:31.280
The power of love for others.

00:48:31.280 --> 00:48:34.280
The power of hope.

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:37.280
May you enter into that rest this week.

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:40.280
And may you find rest for your souls.

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:46.280
Thank you, Greg,

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:48.280
for that message of love

00:48:48.280 --> 00:48:50.280
and the call to justice

00:48:50.280 --> 00:48:52.280
that overflows from rest with Jesus.

00:48:52.280 --> 00:48:54.280
Thank you so much.

00:48:54.280 --> 00:48:58.280
We are going to now partake in communion

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:00.280
by the table.

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.280
We do that here with an open table,

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:04.280
meaning anybody who wants to participate

00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:06.280
is more than welcome to.

00:49:06.280 --> 00:49:08.280
The way we do it is we form a single line

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:10.280
down the middle starting at the front

00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:12.280
working our way back.

00:49:12.280 --> 00:49:14.280
You'll notice that there is both red and white.

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:16.280
The red is wine,

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:18.280
and the white is grape juice.

00:49:18.280 --> 00:49:20.280
And lastly,

00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:22.280
no one will lead you through partaking it.

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:24.280
You can do so on your own.

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:26.280
You can come kneel.

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:28.280
You can go back to your seats.

00:49:28.280 --> 00:49:30.280
Whatever you feel like to do.

00:49:30.280 --> 00:49:32.280
So,

00:49:32.280 --> 00:49:34.280
transition.

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:36.280
This is the table,

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:38.280
not of the church.

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:40.280
This is the table,

00:49:40.280 --> 00:49:42.280
not of the church,

00:49:42.280 --> 00:49:44.280
but of the Lord.

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:46.280
It is made ready for those who love Him

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:48.280
and for those who want to love Him more.

00:49:48.280 --> 00:49:50.280
So come,

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:52.280
you who have much faith

00:49:52.280 --> 00:49:54.280
and you who have little.

00:49:54.280 --> 00:49:56.280
You who have been here often

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:58.280
and you who have not been here long.

00:49:58.280 --> 00:50:00.280
You who have tried to follow

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:02.280
and you who have failed.

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:04.280
Come,

00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:06.280
because it is the Lord who invites you.

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:08.280
Come,

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:10.280
come to Him.

